messages.content:
OneDrive's log directory has grown to 18.4 GB. Logs are written continuously and are normally trimmed - a sync error looping overnight produces exactly this pattern, with space vanishing while no user file is created.
Commands used:
- Get-Volume C | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\' -Directory -Force -EA SilentlyContinue | ForEach-Object { [pscustomobject]@{Name=$_.Name; GB=[math]::Round((Get-ChildItem $_.FullName -Recurse -File -EA SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum).Sum/1GB,1)} } | Sort-Object GB -Descending | Select-Object -First 3
- Get-ChildItem "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Microsoft\OneDrive\logs" -Recurse -File -EA SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
Recommendation: Clear the OneDrive logs, then find the sync error that is generating them - deleting logs without fixing the loop just delays it. The underlying cause is usually a single file OneDrive cannot process, which will be named repeatedly in the most recent log.

Chrome was holding ~2.8 GB across many tabs on an 8 GB machine with only 0.6 GB free, causing heavy paging and slowness. The system itself is healthy; the load is application-driven.
Commands used:
- Get-Process | Sort-Object WorkingSet64 -Descending | Select-Object -First 5 Name, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}, @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}
Recommendation: Close unused Chrome tabs or install a tab-suspender, and consider upgrading to 16 GB RAM for comfortable multitasking.

0x8007000E is an out-of-memory HRESULT; the process is 32-bit and has exhausted its 2 GB address space while the machine still has ample free RAM.
Commands used:
- Get-Process LegacyApp | Select-Object @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}
Recommendation: Use a 64-bit build of the application if one exists, or reduce the working set (smaller datasets, fewer open documents); adding RAM cannot raise a 32-bit process's address space limit.

The model's parameter count requires more RAM than the machine has available once the OS and other applications are accounted for, so loading fails despite the download succeeding.
Commands used:
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}, @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}
- Get-ChildItem "$env:USERPROFILE\.ollama\models" -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
Recommendation: Use a smaller or more heavily quantized model variant that fits available memory, close other applications before loading, or move the workload to a machine with more RAM/VRAM.

One application is holding 6.8 GB and 48,000 handles after a few hours. A handle count climbing into the tens of thousands alongside working set growth is a resource leak in that application, not memory pressure caused by Windows.
Commands used:
- Get-Process | Sort-Object WorkingSet64 -Descending | Select-Object -First 3 Name, Id, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
- Get-Process -Id 7204 | Select-Object Handles, NonpagedSystemMemorySize64, StartTime
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB)}}
Recommendation: Restart that application when it grows rather than the whole machine, and report the handle leak to its vendor with the counts. Adding RAM would extend the time between restarts without fixing anything.

The faulting version is 10.0.10586 while System32 holds 10.0.22621 - the application is loading an old ucrtbase.dll from its own folder. That was tolerable before; the update changed the other CRT pieces it must match, and mixing versions produced a security-check failure (0xc0000409).
Commands used:
- Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Application'; Id=1000} -MaxEvents 1 | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Message
- Get-Item 'C:\Windows\System32\ucrtbase.dll' | Select-Object @{N='Ver';E={$_.VersionInfo.FileVersion}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Apps\Legacy' -Filter 'ucrtbase.dll' -EA SilentlyContinue | Select-Object FullName, @{N='Ver';E={$_.VersionInfo.FileVersion}}
Recommendation: Delete the local ucrtbase.dll so the system one is used. Shipping a private copy of the Universal CRT is unsupported - it must match the rest of the CRT that Windows services independently, and it will break again at the next update.

Committed memory is within 5% of the commit limit, and the paging rate spikes from 18 to over 3,000 pages per second during the freezes. The machine has run out of memory to commit and is thrashing the page file - that stall is the whole system waiting on disk.
Commands used:
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}, @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}
- Get-Counter '\Memory\Committed Bytes','\Memory\Commit Limit' -MaxSamples 1 | Select-Object -ExpandProperty CounterSamples | Select-Object Path, CookedValue
- Get-Counter '\Memory\Pages/sec' -SampleInterval 1 -MaxSamples 3 | ForEach-Object { [int]$_.CounterSamples.CookedValue }
Recommendation: 8 GB is the constraint here, not any single application. Increasing the page file will stop the hard stops but the machine will still be slow; more RAM is the real fix. Closing browser tabs before heavy work buys time in the meantime.

32-bit Office is limited to roughly 2 GB of addressable memory per process regardless of installed RAM, so large models hit the ceiling well before the machine does.
Commands used:
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}
- Get-Process EXCEL | Select-Object @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
Recommendation: Migrate to 64-bit Office (requires uninstalling 32-bit first and checking add-in compatibility), or reduce the model's footprint with Power Pivot/data model instead of raw sheets.

The user temp folder held ~3 GB of junk. Clearing it (safe, in-use files are skipped) freed space, bringing C: back to 11 GB free.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem $env:TEMP -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
- Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\*" -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
- Get-PSDrive C | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.Free/1GB)}}
Recommendation: Run Disk Cleanup / Storage Sense for Windows Update leftovers to reclaim more.

There are 1,204 alternate data streams holding 8.4 GB alongside the 18.4 GB of visible file data. Explorer counts them in the folder size but does not list them - they are commonly left by sync tools, thumbnail caches and Zone.Identifier marks on downloaded files.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem 'D:\Projects' -Recurse -File | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='LogicalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
- (Get-Item 'D:\Projects').GetDirectories().Count; fsutil volume diskfree D:
- Get-ChildItem 'D:\Projects' -Recurse -File | Get-Item -Stream * -EA SilentlyContinue | Where-Object Stream -ne ':$DATA' | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
Recommendation: The Zone.Identifier streams are safe to remove, but check what created the large ones before deleting - some applications store real data in streams. Copying the folder to a FAT32 or exFAT volume drops all streams silently, which is a common way to lose them by accident.

Committed memory is only about 9.8 GB of 31.9 GB; the rest of the reported usage is standby cache, which Windows fills deliberately with recently used data and releases instantly when an application needs it. There is no memory pressure here.
Commands used:
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}, @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}
- Get-Counter '\Memory\Standby Cache Normal Priority Bytes','\Memory\Committed Bytes' -MaxSamples 1 | Select-Object -ExpandProperty CounterSamples | Select-Object Path, CookedValue
- Get-Process | Sort-Object WorkingSet64 -Descending | Select-Object -First 3 Name, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
Recommendation: No action is required, and tools that 'free' memory are counterproductive because they discard useful cache. Watch committed bytes and the hard fault rate instead of the headline percentage if you want a real memory-pressure indicator.

Error 0x800F0922 with 12 MB free on the EFI system partition is the classic combination. The C drive has plenty of room, but the update needs to stage boot files on the tiny system partition and cannot - so it installs, fails at the boot stage, and rolls back.
Commands used:
- Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-WindowsUpdateClient'; Id=20} -MaxEvents 2 | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Message
- Get-Partition | Where-Object Type -eq 'System' | Select-Object Size, @{N='FreeMB';E={[int]((Get-Volume -Partition $_).SizeRemaining/1MB)}}
- Get-Volume C | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
Recommendation: Clear old language folders and stale boot entries from the EFI partition to reclaim space. Do not delete files there indiscriminately - removing the wrong ones leaves the machine unbootable. Enlarging the partition is the durable fix and needs to be done from recovery media.
